# DNS zone verification

A new `nemau_dns_zone` is created with status `PENDING_VERIFICATION` and serves
nothing until a TXT record, given by its `verification_record_name` and
`verification_record_value`, is visible at the domain's current DNS host.
`nemau_dns_zone_verification` makes the apply wait for that record, like
`aws_acm_certificate_validation`: it asks the platform to check the record
every 30 seconds until the check passes or its `timeout` (default `45m`)
expires. It creates nothing, and destroying it leaves the zone verified.

Record sets are refused on a pending zone. Take their `zone_id` from
`nemau_dns_zone_verification.<name>.zone_id` rather than from the zone, so that
Terraform creates them after the verification.

```terraform
resource "nemau_dns_zone" "main" {
  name  = "example.com."
  email = "hostmaster@example.com"
}

resource "nemau_dns_zone_verification" "main" {
  zone_id = nemau_dns_zone.main.id
}

resource "nemau_dns_record_set" "www" {
  zone_id = nemau_dns_zone_verification.main.zone_id
  name    = "www.example.com."
  type    = "A"
  records = ["203.0.113.10"]
}
```

The API stops returning the verification record once the zone is active. The
provider keeps the last values read instead of clearing them, and `status`
keeps its value across in-place updates until the next refresh. As a result,
none of these attributes produces a diff after the verification, on the zone or
on a TXT record declared from them with another provider. They are null on a
zone imported after it became active.

The [DNS example](https://github.com/nemau-cloud/terraform-provider-nemau/tree/main/examples/dns)
also shows how to publish the TXT record with another provider, or by hand in
two applies.
